%[text] # Magnetic Dipoles
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:..\\MainMenu.m)
%[text] From refrigerator magnets to Earth's magnetic field, magnetic dipoles are fundamental to understanding magnetism. In this script, we'll explore how magnetic dipoles behave, interact with external fields, and generate their own magnetic fields.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:8404)
%%
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:8785) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:4788) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2 hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8F8A032D] ![Warning symbol](text:image:0337)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and will generate errors if run out of order.
%[text] The ![Try this icon](text:image:7ebd)  and  ![Exercise icon](text:image:4e65) icons refer to two different types of interactive activities that you will find in this script. The ![Try this icon](text:image:2fc1)  usually indicates an interaction where you will explore the visualization of some concept introduced in this script. The ![Exercise icon](text:image:871c) interactions are designed to challenge your understanding of those concepts and may be used for grading and completion checks by your professor.
%%
%[text] ## Fundamentals of Magnetic Dipoles
%%
%[text] ### What is a Magnetic Dipole?
%[text] A magnetic dipole is a magnetic system with a north-seeking side and a south-seeking side, much like a small bar magnet. Unlike electric charges, magnetic poles have not been observed in isolation, so a north pole always appears with a south pole. This makes magnetic dipoles similar to, but not identical with, [electric dipoles](file:.\\ElectricFieldAndCharge.m).
%[text] The simplest magnetic dipole is a small current loop. When electric current flows in a circular path, it creates a dipole-like magnetic field pattern. Far from the loop, this pattern resembles the field of a tiny bar magnet. This is why we describe magnetic dipoles using the magnetic dipole moment $\\vec{m}$:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\vec{m} = I A \\hat{n} \\qquad (1)$
%[text] Where:
%[text] - $\\vec{m}$ is the magnetic dipole moment vector (in ampere-square meters, A·m²),
%[text] - $I$ is the current flowing through the loop (in amperes, A),
%[text] - $A$ is the area enclosed by the current loop (in square meters, m²),
%[text] - $\\hat{n}$ is the unit vector perpendicular to the loop plane, following the right-hand rule. \
%[text] Recall that the area can take familiar geometric forms, such as $A = ab$ for a rectangular loop with sides $a$ and $b$, or $A = \\pi r^2$ for a circular loop with radius $r$.
%[text] ![](text:image:323d) **Try.** Click the button below to compare the magnetic field from a bar magnet with the field from a current loop. Look for where the field leaves, where it returns, and how the two patterns are similar.
  %[control:button:8cf4]{"position":[1,2]}
CompareMagneticFields();

function CompareMagneticFields()

figure('Color', 'w', 'Position', [100, 100, 700, 450]);
t = tiledlayout(1, 2, 'TileSpacing', 'Compact');

% Grid for field computation
n = 50;
[x, z] = meshgrid(linspace(-3, 3, n), linspace(-3, 3, n));

% Dipole (bar magnet) oriented along +z at origin (approximation)
r = sqrt(x.^2 + z.^2);
rmin = 0.2;
r(r < rmin) = NaN; % avoid singularity
bx_mag = 3 * x .* z ./ r.^5;
bz_mag = (3 * z.^2 - r.^2) ./ r.^5;

% Current loop parameters
a = 1;
rho = abs(x);

% Compute elliptic integrals parameter k^2, ensure valid range [0,1]
k2 = (4 * a .* rho) ./ ((a + rho).^2 + z.^2);
k2 = min(max(k2, 0), 1);

% ellipke accepts array input; it returns K and E of same size
[K, E] = ellipke(k2);

% Auxiliary terms for loop field (Biot-Savart result in cylindrical coords)
den = (a - rho).^2 + z.^2;
den(den == 0) = NaN;
sqrtterm = sqrt((a + rho).^2 + z.^2);

bz_loop = (1 ./ (pi * sqrtterm)) .* (K + (a.^2 - rho.^2 - z.^2) ./ den .* E);
br_loop = (z ./ (rho .* pi .* sqrtterm)) .* (-K + (a.^2 + rho.^2 + z.^2) ./ den .* E);
br_loop(rho == 0) = 0;

% Convert radial component to Cartesian x-component (sign handles direction)
bx_loop = br_loop .* sign(x);

% Reduce field line density
dens = 0.8;

% Left: Bar magnet (dipole) with rectangular halves drawn and labeled
ax1 = nexttile;
streamslice(ax1, x, z, bx_mag, bz_mag, dens);
hold(ax1, "on");

% Local polygons for left (S) and right (N) halves (in magnet-local coords)
% Define top (N) and bottom (S) halves (magnet oriented along +z with N on top)
north_poly  = [ -0.6, 0;
    -0.6,  1;
     0.6,  1;
     0.6,  0 ];
south_poly = [ -0.6, -1;
    -0.6,  0;
     0.6,  0;
     0.6, -1];

% Determine colors: prefer provided MagnetColors, else use axes ColorOrder via SeriesIndex
co = get(ax1, "ColorOrder");
colorS = co(mod(0, size(co,1)) + 1, :); % first color for S (bottom)
colorN = co(mod(1, size(co,1)) + 1, :); % second color for N (top)

% Draw halves (use colors determined above)
patch(ax1, north_poly(:,1), north_poly(:,2), colorN, "EdgeColor", "k", "LineWidth", 1.2); % top = N
patch(ax1, south_poly(:,1), south_poly(:,2), colorS, "EdgeColor", "k", "LineWidth", 1.2); % bottom = S

% Pole labels centered on each half
north_center = mean(north_poly,1);
south_center = mean(south_poly,1);
text(ax1, north_center(1), north_center(2), "N", "Color", "w", "FontWeight", "bold", "FontSize", 14, ...
    "HorizontalAlignment", "center", "VerticalAlignment", "middle");
text(ax1, south_center(1), south_center(2), "S", "Color", "w", "FontWeight", "bold", "FontSize", 14, ...
    "HorizontalAlignment", "center", "VerticalAlignment", "middle");

title(ax1, "Bar Magnet");
axis(ax1, "equal");
axis(ax1, [-3 3 -3 3]);
axis(ax1, "off");

% Right: Current loop
ax2 = nexttile;
streamslice(ax2, x, z, bx_loop, bz_loop, dens);
hold(ax2, "on");

Radius = a; % match naming in requested snippet
% Draw loop line on top of symbols
h_loop = plot(ax2, [-Radius Radius],[0 0], LineStyle="-", SeriesIndex=2, LineWidth=1.5);

% Left side: current INTO page (represented by 'x' marker)
h_x = scatter(ax2, -Radius, 0, 250, "x", LineWidth=3, SeriesIndex=2);

% Right side: current OUT OF page (represented by concentric 'o' markers)
h_o1 = scatter(ax2, Radius, 0, 200, "o", LineWidth=2.5, SeriesIndex=2);
scatter(ax2, Radius, 0, 30, "o", "filled", SeriesIndex=2);

% Create a legend indicating the loop and current direction symbols
legend(ax2, [h_loop, h_x, h_o1], {"Current loop", "Current into page", "Current out of page"}, ...
    "Location", "northeast");

title(ax2, "Current Loop");
axis(ax2, "equal");
axis(ax2, [-3 3 -3 3]);
axis(ax2, "off");

title(t, "Comparison of Magnetic Field Lines");

end

%%
%[text] ### Real-World Applications
%[text] Magnetic dipoles appear in many familiar systems, from compasses to motors:
%[text] - **Compass needles** are magnetic dipoles that align with Earth's magnetic field
%[text] - **MRI machines** rely primarily on the nuclear magnetic dipole moments of hydrogen nuclei (protons) in your body
%[text] - **Hard disk drives** store data using billions of tiny magnetic dipoles
%[text] - **Electric motors** use magnetic torque to convert electrical energy to mechanical energy, while **generators** use Faraday induction to convert mechanical energy to electrical energy
%[text] - **Atoms and molecules** have magnetic dipole moments due to electron spin and orbital motion \
%[text] These examples all rely on the same idea: a magnetic dipole interacts with an external magnetic field.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Plastic compass, credit: Wikimedia Commons](text:image:83d1)![MRI machine at a medical facility, credit: US Navy / Wikimedia Commons](text:image:9510)
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Inside of a hard disk drive, credit: Wikimedia Commons](text:image:7bc5)![](text:image:68c3)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Image credits and licenses: Wikimedia Commons (see details* [here](internal:M_36e6)*)*
%%
%[text] ### Visualizing Dipole Fields
%[text] Now that you have seen the similarity between a bar magnet and a current loop, let's explore the dipole field in more detail. Magnetic field lines form closed loops; outside a magnet or loop they leave the north side and return to the south side, then continue through the interior.
%[text] By default, the visualization shows a side view of a circular current loop. The loop appears as a horizontal line because we are looking at its edge, with current flowing into the page (⊗) on the left and out of the page (⊙) on the right.
%[text] ![](text:image:69e5) ![Try this icon](text:image:6b32) **Try.** Click the button to visualize the magnetic field pattern of the current loop. Toggle the 3D view checkbox to see the field in three dimensions.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:0627) **Pro-tip.** In 3D mode, click and drag to rotate the view and explore the field from different angles.
  %[control:button:518d22a]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeDipoleField(false); %[control:checkbox:6b24]{"position":[22,27]}

function ax = VisualizeDipoleField(Show3D,options)

arguments
    Show3D (1,1) logical = false
    options.AngleDeg (1,1) double = 0
    options.ShowFieldLine (1,1) logical = true
    options.ShowExternalField (1,1) logical = false
end

    figure('Color', 'w', 'Position', [100, 100, 700, 450]);
    ax = axes();
    set(ax,"XLim",[-3 3],"YLim",[-3 3],"ZLim",[-3 3],"PlotBoxAspectRatio",[1 1 1],"Visible","off")
    
    if Show3D
        view(ax,3)
    end
    hold(ax,"on")
    
    ax = AddFrame(ax,Show3D,options.AngleDeg,options.ShowFieldLine,options.ShowExternalField);

    hold(ax,"off")

end


function ax = AddFrame(ax, Show3D, AngleDeg, ShowFieldLine,ShowExternalField)

arguments
    ax (1,1) matlab.graphics.axis.Axes
    Show3D (1,1) logical = false
    AngleDeg (1,1) double = 0
    ShowFieldLine (1,1) logical = true
    ShowExternalField (1,1) logical = false
end

% Setup:
Radius = 1;

% Grid for field computation
n = 50;
[x, z] = meshgrid(linspace(-6, 6, n), linspace(-6, 6, n));
rho = abs(x);
k2 = (4 * Radius .* rho) ./ ((Radius + rho).^2 + z.^2);
k2 = min(max(k2, 0), 1);
[K, E] = ellipke(k2);
den = (Radius - rho).^2 + z.^2;
den(den == 0) = NaN;
sqrtterm = sqrt((Radius + rho).^2 + z.^2);
bz_loop = (1 ./ (pi * sqrtterm)) .* (K + (Radius.^2 - rho.^2 - z.^2) ./ den .* E);
br_loop = (z ./ (rho .* pi .* sqrtterm)) .* (-K + (Radius.^2 + rho.^2 + z.^2) ./ den .* E);
br_loop(rho == 0) = 0;
bx_loop = br_loop .* sign(x);

% Plot the field line
fl = streamslice(ax, x, z, bx_loop, bz_loop, 0.8);

% Create grid for field visualization
if Show3D
    
    
    if ShowFieldLine
        rotate(fl,[1 0 0],90);
        flcp = copyobj(fl,ax);
        rotate(flcp,[0 0 1], 90);
        rotate(fl,[1 0 0],AngleDeg)
        rotate(flcp,[1 0 0],AngleDeg)
    else
        delete(fl)
    end

    % Draw the loop
    Theta = linspace(0,2*pi);
    loop = plot3(ax,Radius*cos(Theta),Radius*sin(Theta),0*Theta,LineStyle="-",SeriesIndex=2, LineWidth=1.5);
    rotate(loop,[1 0 0],AngleDeg)
    
    % Draw moment
    quiver3(ax,0, 0, 0, ...
        0, 2.0*cosd(90+AngleDeg), 2.0*sind(90+AngleDeg),...
        LineWidth=3, MaxHeadSize=1.5, SeriesIndex=3,Alignment="center");
    text(ax,0.2,0.2, 0.3, '$\vec{m}$', FontSize=14, FontWeight='bold',Interpreter='latex')

    if ShowExternalField
        [X,Y,Z] = meshgrid(linspace(-2.5,2.5,3),2.8,linspace(-2.5,2.5,3));
        Bx = 0*X;
        By = Bx - 0.5;
        Bz = Bx;
        quiver3(X,Y,Z,Bx,By,Bz,0.5,...
            LineWidth=3, MaxHeadSize=1.5, SeriesIndex=5)
    end

elseif ~Show3D

    % Draw the loop in (x,z) plane
    plot(ax,[-Radius Radius],[0 0], LineStyle="-", SeriesIndex=2, LineWidth=1.5);
    scatter(ax,-Radius, 0, 250, "x", LineWidth=3, SeriesIndex=2);
    scatter(ax,Radius, 0, 200, "o", LineWidth=2.5, SeriesIndex=2);
    scatter(ax,Radius, 0, 30, "o", "filled", SeriesIndex=2);
    
    % Add magnet representation
    north_poly  = [ -0.6, 1.5; -0.6,  2.5; 0.6,  2.5; 0.6,  1.5 ];
    south_poly = [ -0.6, -2.5; -0.6,  -1.5; 0.6,  -1.5; 0.6, -2.5];
    patch(ax,north_poly(:,1), north_poly(:,2), [0.9 0 0], EdgeColor="k", LineWidth=1.2) % top = N
    patch(ax,south_poly(:,1), south_poly(:,2), [0 0 0.8], EdgeColor="k", LineWidth=1.2) % bottom = S
    north_center = mean(north_poly,1);
    south_center = mean(south_poly,1);
    text(ax,north_center(1), north_center(2), "N", Color="w", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=14, ...
        HorizontalAlignment="center", VerticalAlignment="middle")
    text(ax,south_center(1), south_center(2), "S", Color="w", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=14, ...
        HorizontalAlignment="center", VerticalAlignment="middle")
    
    % Add the moment
    quiver(ax,0, 0, 0, 2.0, 0, LineWidth=3, MaxHeadSize=1.5, SeriesIndex=3,Alignment="center")
    text(ax,0.2, 0.3, '$\vec{m}$', FontSize=14, FontWeight='bold',Interpreter='latex')

    title(ax,"Current Loop")

end
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:4b8b)![Reflect icon](text:image:8491) **Reflect.** Notice that outside the loop, the field lines leave one side and curve around to enter the other side. This is the same dipole-like pattern used to model a small bar magnet. A larger current or loop area gives a larger magnetic dipole moment and a stronger field.
%%
%[text] ## Magnetic Dipoles in External Fields
%%
%[text] ### Torque on a Magnetic Dipole
%[text] When a magnetic dipole is placed in an external magnetic field, it experiences a torque that rotates the dipole toward alignment with the field, just like a compass needle rotating to point north. This same torque is one ingredient in electric motor operation.
%[text] The torque $\\vec{\\tau}$ on a magnetic dipole with moment $\\vec{m}$ in an external magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{m} \\times \\vec{B} \\qquad (2)$
%[text] Where:
%[text] - $\\vec{\\tau}$ is the torque vector (in newton-meters, N·m),
%[text] - $\\vec{m}$ is the magnetic dipole moment (in A·m²),
%[text] - $\\vec{B}$ is the external magnetic field (in teslas, T),
%[text] - $\\times$ denotes the [vector cross product](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/cross.html). \
%[text] The magnitude of the torque is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\tau = m B \\sin\\theta \\qquad (3)$
%[text] where $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\vec{m}$ and $\\vec{B}$, and and $B =  \\|\\vec{B}\\|$ . The torque is maximum when the dipole is perpendicular to the field ($\\theta = 90\\degree $) and zero when it is aligned or anti-aligned ($\\theta = 0\\degree $ or $180\\degree $).
%[text] Before you run the visualization, predict which angle should give the strongest twist and which angles should give no twist. Then compare your prediction with the force arrows.
%[text] ![](text:image:15ba)![Try this icon](text:image:989e) **Try.** Click the button to visualize the torque on the current loop at a representative angle $\\theta$:
  %[control:button:9b2d6640890477]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeTorque(45);

function VisualizeTorque(AngleDeg)
% VisualizeTorque Draws the current loop in an external field showing torque
%   Pedagogical purpose: Help students see how torque varies with angle

figure(Color="w", Position=[100 100 800 450]);
tiledlayout(1, 2, TileSpacing="compact");

% --- Left panel: Loop in external field ---
ax1 = nexttile;
hold(ax1, "on");
axis(ax1, "equal");
axis(ax1, "off");
xlim(ax1, [-2 2]);
ylim(ax1, [-2 2]);

AngleRad = deg2rad(AngleDeg);

% Draw external field arrows (horizontal, pointing right)
[Xq, Yq] = meshgrid(linspace(-1.8, 1.8, 7), linspace(-1.5, 1.5, 5));
quiver(ax1, Xq, Yq, 0.2*ones(size(Xq)), zeros(size(Yq)), 0, ...
    Color=[0.6 0.8 0.6], LineWidth=0.8, AutoScale="off");

% Draw current loop edge-on. AngleDeg is the angle between m and B.
LoopLen = 1.2;
mDir = [cos(AngleRad); sin(AngleRad)];
loopDir = [-sin(AngleRad); cos(AngleRad)];
endpts = loopDir * [-LoopLen/2 LoopLen/2];
plot(ax1, endpts(1,:), endpts(2,:), LineWidth=3, SeriesIndex=2);

% Current direction markers
scatter(ax1, endpts(1,1), endpts(2,1), 250, "x", LineWidth=3, SeriesIndex=2);
scatter(ax1, endpts(1,2), endpts(2,2), 200, "o", LineWidth=2.5, SeriesIndex=2);
scatter(ax1, endpts(1,2), endpts(2,2), 30, "o", "filled", SeriesIndex=2);

% Dipole moment arrow (perpendicular to loop, following right-hand rule)
quiver(ax1, 0, 0, 0.8*mDir(1), 0.8*mDir(2), 0, ...
    LineWidth=3, MaxHeadSize=1.5, SeriesIndex=3, Alignment="center");

% Force arrows on the ends of the loop (creates torque)
if abs(sind(AngleDeg)) > 0.01
    Fscale = 0.6;
    % Current out of page in a rightward B field gives an upward force.
    quiver(ax1, endpts(1,2), endpts(2,2), 0, Fscale, 0, ...
        LineWidth=2.5, MaxHeadSize=1.2, SeriesIndex=4);
    % Current into page in a rightward B field gives a downward force.
    quiver(ax1, endpts(1,1), endpts(2,1), 0, -Fscale, 0, ...
        LineWidth=2.5, MaxHeadSize=1.2, SeriesIndex=4);
end

% Angle arc
arc_rad = 0.5;
tArc = linspace(0, AngleRad, 60);
plot(ax1, arc_rad*cos(tArc), arc_rad*sin(tArc), "k-", LineWidth=1);
text(ax1, 0.55*cos(AngleRad/2)+0.05, 0.55*sin(AngleRad/2), ...
    sprintf("%.0f°", AngleDeg), FontSize=11, FontWeight="bold");

title(ax1, sprintf("Current Loop at \\theta = %d°", AngleDeg), FontSize=12);
legend(ax1, "", "", "", "", "", "m (dipole moment)", "F (force)", Location="best");

% --- Right panel: Torque vs angle curve ---
ax2 = nexttile;
angles = linspace(0, 180, 200);
normTorque = sind(angles);
plot(ax2, angles, normTorque, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=1);
hold(ax2, "on");
plot(ax2, AngleDeg, sind(AngleDeg), "o", MarkerSize=12, ...
    MarkerFaceColor="auto", SeriesIndex=5);
hold(ax2, "off");
xlabel(ax2, "Angle \\theta (degrees)");
ylabel(ax2, "\\tau / (mB)");
title(ax2, "Normalized Torque vs Angle");
grid(ax2, "on");
xlim(ax2, [0 180]);
ylim(ax2, [-0.1 1.1]);
xline(ax2, AngleDeg, "--", SeriesIndex=5, Alpha=0.5);
legend(ax2, "sin(\\theta)", sprintf("Current: %.0f°", AngleDeg), Location="best");

end
%%
%[text] #### Simulate Dipole Dynamics
%[text] Now let's see what happens over time when we release the loop from a tilted position. The torque will cause it to rotate and oscillate before settling into alignment with the field.
%[text] ![](text:image:5950)![Try this icon](text:image:6ed9) **Try.** Click the button below to simulate the dynamics of the same circular current loop starting from a 90° angle. Watch how the oscillation changes as damping removes energy.
  %[control:button:1d6ace5]{"position":[1,2]}
SimulateDipole
function SimulateDipole()
% Use same parameters as the torque diagram (and first visualization)
Current = 1.0;        % A
Radius = 0.08;        % m
Area = pi * Radius^2;
DipoleMoment = Current * Area;
FieldMagnitude = 2; % Tesla

% Physical parameters for dynamics
MomentOfInertia = 1e-5; % kg·m² (small loop)
Damping = 0.05;         % Damping coefficient

% Initial conditions
theta0 = deg2rad(90);
omega0 = 0; % Initial angular velocity

% Time span
tspan = linspace(0, 5);

% Equation of motion: I*d²θ/dt² = -m*B*sin(θ) - damping*dθ/dt
ode = @(t, y) [y(2); ...
    (-DipoleMoment * FieldMagnitude * sin(y(1)) - Damping * y(2)) / MomentOfInertia];

[~, y] = ode45(ode, tspan, [theta0; omega0]);
theta = 90-rad2deg(y(:, 1));

ax = VisualizeDipoleField(true,AngleDeg=0,ShowFieldLine=false,ShowExternalField=true);
drawnow
for i = 2:length(theta)
    delete(ax.Children)
    hold(ax,"on")
    ax = AddFrame(ax,true,theta(i),false,true);
    drawnow
end
end
%[text] ![](text:image:0ffb) **Reflect.** The dipole oscillates before settling into alignment. Damping gradually removes mechanical energy, so each oscillation is smaller than the last. In real systems, this damping could come from air resistance, internal friction, or energy loss to heat.
%%
%[text] ### Potential Energy of a Magnetic Dipole
%[text] Just as a ball on a hill has gravitational potential energy, a magnetic dipole in an external field has magnetic potential energy. The potential energy depends on the dipole's orientation relative to the field:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ U = -\\vec{m} \\cdot \\vec{B} = -mB\\cos\\theta \\qquad (4)$
%[text] Where:
%[text] - $U$ is the magnetic potential energy (in joules, J),
%[text] - $\\vec{m}$ is the magnetic dipole moment (in A·m²),
%[text] - $\\vec{B}$ is the external magnetic field (in T),
%[text] - $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\vec{m}$ and $\\vec{B}$,
%[text] - $\\cdot$ denotes the [vector dot product](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/dot.html). \
%[text] Key observations:
%[text] - Minimum energy ($U = -mB$) when the dipole is aligned with the field ($\\theta = 0\\degree $) $\\rightarrow$ this is the stable equilibrium
%[text] - Maximum energy ($U = +mB$) when the dipole is anti-aligned with the field ($\\theta = 180\\degree $) $\\rightarrow$ this is the unstable equilibrium
%[text] - Zero energy when the dipole is perpendicular to the field ($\\theta = 90\\degree $) \
%[text] ![](text:image:3bfd)![Exercise icon](text:image:19e3) **Exercise 1.** Use the button below to generate three situations and, in each case, identify which energy state it corresponds to. Compare the direction of $\\vec{m}$ with the external field $\\vec{B}$.
  %[control:button:99aa]{"position":[1,2]}
Prob1 = GenerateProblem1();

function y = GenerateProblem1()
y.Order = randperm(3);
Options = ["U = +mB" "U = 0" "U = -mB"];
AngleLabels = [180 90 0];
y.Answer = Options(y.Order);
Angle = [-90 0 90];
Angle = Angle(y.Order);
y.AngleDeg = AngleLabels(y.Order);

figure(Position=[0 0 800 300])
tiledlayout(1,3,"Padding","tight");

ax = nexttile;
set(ax,"XLim",[-3 3],"YLim",[-3 3],"ZLim",[-3 3],"PlotBoxAspectRatio",[1 1 1],"Visible","off")
hold(ax,"on")
view(ax,3)
ax = AddFrame(ax,true,Angle(1),false,true);
hold(ax,"off")
title(sprintf("Left case: \\theta = %d°", y.AngleDeg(1)))

ax = nexttile;
set(ax,"XLim",[-3 3],"YLim",[-3 3],"ZLim",[-3 3],"PlotBoxAspectRatio",[1 1 1],"Visible","off")
hold(ax,"on")
view(ax,3)
ax = AddFrame(ax,true,Angle(2),false,true);
hold(ax,"off")
title(sprintf("Middle case: \\theta = %d°", y.AngleDeg(2)))

ax = nexttile;
set(ax,"XLim",[-3 3],"YLim",[-3 3],"ZLim",[-3 3],"PlotBoxAspectRatio",[1 1 1],"Visible","off")
hold(ax,"on")
view(ax,3)
ax = AddFrame(ax,true,Angle(3),false,true);
hold(ax,"off")
title(sprintf("Right case: \\theta = %d°", y.AngleDeg(3)))
displayFormula('"Use the panel titles to compare the angle between m and B with U = -mB cos(θ)."');
end
%%
if exist("Prob1", "var")
    CheckExercise1(Prob1,"Select","Select","Select")  %[control:dropdown:040d]{"position":[26,34]} %[control:dropdown:078c]{"position":[35,43]} %[control:dropdown:4b4d]{"position":[44,52]}
else
    warning("Click Generate first to create the three cases.")
end
function CheckExercise1(Prob,Ans1,Ans2,Ans3)

% Ensure answers are provided (not left as "Select")
if any(strcmp(Ans1, "Select") | strcmp(Ans2, "Select") | strcmp(Ans3, "Select"))
    displayFormula('"Please select an answer for all cases before checking."');
    return;
end

checkCase("Left", Ans1, Prob.Answer(1), Prob.AngleDeg(1));
checkCase("Middle", Ans2, Prob.Answer(2), Prob.AngleDeg(2));
checkCase("Right", Ans3, Prob.Answer(3), Prob.AngleDeg(3));

end

function checkCase(caseName, answer, correctAnswer, angleDeg)
if answer == correctAnswer
    displayFormula(sprintf('"%s case: Correct. At θ = %d°, %s."', caseName, angleDeg, describeEnergy(angleDeg)));
else
    displayFormula(sprintf('"%s case: Incorrect. Compare θ = %d° with U = -mB cos(θ), then try again."', ...
        caseName, angleDeg));
end
end

function description = describeEnergy(angleDeg)
switch angleDeg
    case 0
        description = "m is aligned with B and U = -mB";
    case 90
        description = "m is perpendicular to B and U = 0";
    case 180
        description = "m is anti-aligned with B and U = +mB";
    otherwise
        description = "use U = -mB cos(θ)";
end
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3ff8)![Try this icon](text:image:97eb) **Try.** Visualize the potential energy as a function of angle to understand why dipoles align with external fields:
  %[control:button:2a50f4]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeDipoleEnergy(0.1,...
    0.7)

function VisualizeDipoleEnergy(DipoleMoment, FieldStrength)
% VisualizeDipoleEnergy Shows potential energy vs angle
%   Pedagogical purpose: Help students understand energy minimization

theta = linspace(-180, 180, 200);
theta_rad = deg2rad(theta);
U = -DipoleMoment * FieldStrength * cos(theta_rad);

figure(Position=[100 100 800 500])
plot(theta, U/max(U), LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=1);
hold on;

% Mark equilibrium points
ax = gca;
stableColor = ax.ColorOrder(2,:);
unstableColor = ax.ColorOrder(4,:);
plot(0, -DipoleMoment*FieldStrength/max(U), 'o', ...
     MarkerSize=12, MarkerFaceColor=stableColor, SeriesIndex=2);
plot([-180, 180], [DipoleMoment*FieldStrength/max(U), DipoleMoment*FieldStrength/max(U)], 'o', ...
     MarkerSize=12, MarkerFaceColor=unstableColor, SeriesIndex=3);

% Mark zero energy
yline(0, '--', 'U = 0', SeriesIndex=4);

hold off;
xlabel('Angle θ (degrees)');
ylabel('Potential Energy U (mB)');
title('Magnetic Potential Energy');
legend('U(θ)', 'Stable (aligned)', 'Unstable (anti-aligned)', 'Zero energy', ...
       Location='best');
grid on;
xlim([-180, 180]);
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7bf2) **Reflect.** The energy landscape shows why aligned dipoles are stable: the aligned orientation has the lowest potential energy.
%[text] - At $\\theta = 0\\degree $, the potential energy is at its minimum ($U = -mB$): the dipole is aligned with the field. This is the stable equilibrium, like a valley, any small push will bring the dipole back.
%[text] - At $\\theta = 180\\degree $, the potential energy is at its maximum ($U = +mB$): the dipole is anti-aligned. This is the unstable equilibrium, like a hilltop, any small perturbation will cause the dipole to rotate away.
%[text] - At $\\theta = 90\\degree $, the energy is zero. The torque is at its maximum here, driving the dipole toward alignment. \
%[text] ![](text:image:42e1) **Try.** Click the button below for a refresher on stable vs unstable equilibrium.
  %[control:button:3835]{"position":[1,2]}
EquilibriumDiagram

function EquilibriumDiagram()
figure('Position', [100, 100, 800, 350]);
layout = tiledlayout(1, 2, TileSpacing="compact");
title(layout, "Equilibrium Types", FontSize=14, FontWeight="bold");
colors = colororder;

% --- Subplot 1: Stable Equilibrium (forces pushing OUTWARD) ---
nexttile;
hold on;
axis equal;
xlim([-4, 4]);
ylim([-1.5, 1.5]);

bar_width = 3;
bar_height = 0.3;
rectangle('Position', [-bar_width/2, -bar_height/2, bar_width, bar_height], ...
    'FaceColor', [0.7 0.7 0.7], 'EdgeColor', 'k', 'LineWidth', 2);

plot(0, 0, 'o', 'MarkerSize', 10, 'MarkerFaceColor', colors(2,:), ...
    'MarkerEdgeColor', 'k');

% Force arrows pointing OUTWARD (stable in this refresher diagram)
quiver(-3, 0, -0.8, 0, 'Color', colors(2,:), 'LineWidth', 3, 'MaxHeadSize', 1.5);
quiver(3, 0, 0.8, 0, 'Color', colors(2,:), 'LineWidth', 3, 'MaxHeadSize', 1.5);

plot(-3, 0, 'o', 'MarkerSize', 20, 'MarkerFaceColor', [0.6 0.8 1], ...
    'MarkerEdgeColor', colors(2,:));
plot(3, 0, 'o', 'MarkerSize', 20, 'MarkerFaceColor', [0.6 0.8 1], ...
    'MarkerEdgeColor', colors(2,:));

title('Stable Equilibrium', 'FontSize', 14, 'FontWeight', 'bold');
text(0, -1.0, '← * →  Stable response', ...
    'HorizontalAlignment', 'center', 'FontSize', 10, 'FontAngle', 'italic');
axis off;

% --- Subplot 2: Unstable Equilibrium (forces pushing INWARD) ---
nexttile;
hold on;
axis equal;
xlim([-4, 4]);
ylim([-1.5, 1.5]);

rectangle('Position', [-bar_width/2, -bar_height/2, bar_width, bar_height], ...
    'FaceColor', [0.7 0.7 0.7], 'EdgeColor', 'k', 'LineWidth', 2);

plot(0, 0, 'o', 'MarkerSize', 10, 'MarkerFaceColor', colors(4,:), ...
    'MarkerEdgeColor', 'k');

% Force arrows pointing INWARD (unstable in this refresher diagram)
quiver(-3, 0, 0.8, 0, 'Color', colors(4,:), 'LineWidth', 3, 'MaxHeadSize', 1.5);
quiver(3, 0, -0.8, 0, 'Color', colors(4,:), 'LineWidth', 3, 'MaxHeadSize', 1.5);

plot(-3, 0, 'o', 'MarkerSize', 20, 'MarkerFaceColor', [1 0.85 0.7], ...
    'MarkerEdgeColor', colors(4,:));
plot(3, 0, 'o', 'MarkerSize', 20, 'MarkerFaceColor', [1 0.85 0.7], ...
    'MarkerEdgeColor', colors(4,:));

title('Unstable Equilibrium', 'FontSize', 14, 'FontWeight', 'bold');
text(0, -1.0, '→ * ←  Unstable response', ...
    'HorizontalAlignment', 'center', 'FontSize', 10, 'FontAngle', 'italic');
axis off;
end
%%
%[text] ### Worked Example: Bar Magnet as a Dipole
%[text] Let's work through a complete example to see how magnetic dipole concepts apply to a real bar magnet.
%[text] **Problem:** A bar magnet has a magnetic dipole moment of $m = 2.5 \\text{ A\\cdot m}^2$ and is placed in a uniform external magnetic field of $B = 0.30 \\text{ T}$. Initially, the magnet is oriented at $45\\degree $ to the field.
  %[control:button:09ca]{"position":[1,2]}
DrawBarMagnet(45);% Draw and display bar magnet diagram at specified angle

function DrawBarMagnet(angleDeg)
% DrawBarMagnet Draws a bar magnet tilted by angleDeg in a uniform field.
%   DrawBarMagnet(angleDeg) creates a clean figure showing a two-colored
%   bar magnet (S blue, N red), the dipole moment vector, uniform field
%   arrows to the right, and an annotated angle arc.

% Validate input
if nargin < 1 || isempty(angleDeg)
    angleDeg = 45;
end

angle_rad = deg2rad(angleDeg);

fig2 = figure(Name="Bar Magnet Diagram", Color="w", Position=[150 150 700 400]);
ax2 = axes(Parent=fig2);
hold(ax2, "on");
axis(ax2, "equal");
axis(ax2, "off");

% Magnet geometry
mag_center = [0, 0];
mag_length = 1;      % total length
mag_width = 0.25;    % thickness
halfL = mag_length/2;
halfW = mag_width/2;

% Rotation matrix
R = [cos(angle_rad) -sin(angle_rad); sin(angle_rad) cos(angle_rad)];

% Local polygons for left (S) and right (N) halves
left_poly_local  = [ -halfL, -halfW;
                      0,     -halfW;
                      0,      halfW;
                    -halfL,  halfW ];
right_poly_local = [  0,     -halfW;
                      halfL, -halfW;
                      halfL,  halfW;
                       0,      halfW ];

left_poly  = (R * left_poly_local')' + mag_center;
right_poly = (R * right_poly_local')' + mag_center;

% Draw halves
patch(ax2, left_poly(:,1), left_poly(:,2), [0 0.4470 0.7410], EdgeColor="k", LineWidth=1.2);
patch(ax2, right_poly(:,1), right_poly(:,2), [0.8500 0.3250 0.0980], EdgeColor="k", LineWidth=1.2);

% Pole labels
left_center = mean(left_poly,1);
right_center = mean(right_poly,1);
text(ax2, left_center(1), left_center(2), "S", Color="w", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=14, ...
    HorizontalAlignment="center", VerticalAlignment="middle");
text(ax2, right_center(1), right_center(2), "N", Color="w", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=14, ...
    HorizontalAlignment="center", VerticalAlignment="middle");

% Dipole moment vector (from S to N)
quiver(ax2, left_center(1), left_center(2), right_center(1)-left_center(1), ...
    right_center(2)-left_center(2), 0, "MaxHeadSize",0.8, "Color",[0 0 0], "LineWidth",1.5);

% Uniform external field arrows (rightward)
[Xq,Yq] = meshgrid(linspace(-1.5,1.5,9), linspace(-0.9,0.9,5));
U = ones(size(Xq)); V = zeros(size(Yq));
quiver(ax2, Xq, Yq, 0.25*U, 0.25*V, 0, "Color",[0 0.6 0], "LineWidth",1, "AutoScale","off");

% Angle arc annotation between field (0 rad) and magnet orientation
arc_rad = 0.45;
t = linspace(0, angle_rad, 60);
arc_x = arc_rad * cos(t);
arc_y = arc_rad * sin(t);
plot(ax2, arc_x, arc_y, "k-", "LineWidth",1);

% Angle label
ang_label_pos = [arc_rad*cos(angle_rad/2), arc_rad*sin(angle_rad/2)];
text(ax2, ang_label_pos(1)+0.05, ang_label_pos(2)+0.02, sprintf("%d°", angleDeg), ...
    "FontSize",12, "FontWeight","bold");

% Title and view limits
title(ax2, "Bar Magnet (m) at 45° in Uniform Field B", FontSize=12, FontWeight="bold");
pad = 0.6;
xlim(ax2, [-mag_length/1.5-pad, mag_length/1.5+pad]);
ylim(ax2, [-mag_length/1.5-pad, mag_length/1.5+pad]);

end
%[text] (a) What is the initial torque on the magnet?
%[text] (b) What is the initial potential energy?
%[text] (c) What is the final potential energy when the magnet aligns with the field?
%[text] (d) How much work must be done to rotate the magnet from aligned to perpendicular?
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] **(a) Initial torque:**
%[text] Using equation (3):
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\tau = mB\\sin\\theta = (2.5)(0.30)\\sin(45\\degree ) = 0.53 \\text{ N\\cdot m}$
%[text] **(b) Initial potential energy:**
%[text] Using equation (4):
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ U\_i = -mB\\cos\\theta = -(2.5)(0.30)\\cos(45\\degree ) = -0.53 \\text{ J}$
%[text] **(c) Final potential energy (aligned):**
%[text] When aligned, $\\theta = 0\\degree $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ U\_f = -mB\\cos(0\\degree ) = -(2.5)(0.30)(1) = -0.75 \\text{ J}$
%[text] **(d) Work to rotate to perpendicular:**
%[text] When perpendicular, $\\theta = 90\\degree $, so $U = 0$. Starting from aligned:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ W = \\Delta U = U\_f - U\_i = 0 - (-0.75) = 0.75 \\text{ J}$
%[text] We must do $0.75 \\text{ J}$ of work against the magnetic torque to rotate the magnet to the perpendicular position.
%%
%[text] ### Practice Problems
%%
%[text] #### Calculate Torque
%[text] ![](text:image:526a)![Exercise icon](text:image:65c4) **Exercise 2.** Use the `Generate Problem` button to generate a problem and the dropdown below to select your answer for the torque on a magnetic dipole.
  %[control:button:5a6ff96]{"position":[1,2]}
Prob2 = GenerateProblemStatement2;

function Prob = GenerateProblemStatement2()
% Generate random problem parameters
ProblemTypes = ["Rectangular", "Circular"];
Prob.Type = ProblemTypes(randi(2));

switch Prob.Type
    case "Rectangular"
        Prob.Current = randi([5, 20]); % A
        Prob.Width = randi([5, 15])/100; % m
        Prob.Height = randi([5, 15])/100; % m
        Prob.Angle = randi([30, 60]); % degrees
        Prob.Field = randi([3, 8])/10; % T
        
        Area = Prob.Width * Prob.Height;
        DipoleMoment = Prob.Current * Area;
        Torque = DipoleMoment * Prob.Field * sind(Prob.Angle);
        
        displayFormula(['"A rectangular current loop carries a current of ', ...
                      sprintf('%d A', Prob.Current), '."'])
        displayFormula(['"The loop has dimensions ', ...
                      sprintf('%.2f m', Prob.Width), ' × ', sprintf('%.2f m', Prob.Height), '."'])
        displayFormula(['"The loop''s magnetic dipole moment, perpendicular to the loop, makes an angle of ', ...
                      sprintf('%d', Prob.Angle), '° with a uniform magnetic field of ', ...
                      sprintf('%.2f T', Prob.Field), '."'])
        displayFormula('"Calculate the magnitude of the torque on the loop."');
        
    case "Circular"
        Prob.Current = randi([5, 20]); % A
        Prob.Radius = randi([5, 15])/100; % m
        Prob.Angle = randi([30, 60]); % degrees
        Prob.Field = randi([3, 8])/10; % T
        
        Area = pi * Prob.Radius^2;
        DipoleMoment = Prob.Current * Area;
        Torque = DipoleMoment * Prob.Field * sind(Prob.Angle);
        
        displayFormula(['"A circular current loop carries a current of ', ...
                      sprintf('%d A', Prob.Current), '."'])
        displayFormula(['"The loop has a radius of ', ...
                      sprintf('%.2f m', Prob.Radius), '."'])
        displayFormula(['"The loop''s magnetic dipole moment, perpendicular to the loop, makes an angle of ', ...
                      sprintf('%d', Prob.Angle), '° with a uniform magnetic field of ', ...
                      sprintf('%.2f T', Prob.Field), '."']);
        displayFormula('"Calculate the magnitude of the torque on the loop."');
end

% Generate multiple choice options
CorrectAnswer = Torque;
MaxTorque = DipoleMoment * Prob.Field;
Options = makeUniqueTorqueOptions(CorrectAnswer, MaxTorque);

% Shuffle options
ShuffleIdx = randperm(4);
Options = Options(ShuffleIdx);
CorrectIdx = ShuffleIdx == 1;
Letters = ["A", "B", "C", "D"];
Prob.CorrectAnswer = Letters(CorrectIdx);
Prob.CorrectValue = CorrectAnswer;

displayFormula('" "');
for i = 1:4
    displayFormula(sprintf('"%s: %.4f N·m"', Letters(i), Options(i)));
end

Prob.ID = "Ex2";
end

function Options = makeUniqueTorqueOptions(correctAnswer, maxTorque)
Options = correctAnswer;
CandidateOptions = [ ...
    0.40 * correctAnswer, ...
    0.65 * correctAnswer, ...
    1.35 * correctAnswer, ...
    1.75 * correctAnswer, ...
    maxTorque, ...
    2.20 * correctAnswer];

for candidate = CandidateOptions
    Options = appendRoundedUniqueOption(Options, candidate);
    if numel(Options) == 4
        return
    end
end

scaleFactor = 2.50;
while numel(Options) < 4
    Options = appendRoundedUniqueOption(Options, scaleFactor * correctAnswer);
    scaleFactor = scaleFactor + 0.35;
end
end

function Options = appendRoundedUniqueOption(options, candidate)
Options = options;
if candidate > 0 && ~ismember(round(candidate, 4), round(Options, 4))
    Options(end + 1) = candidate;
end
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:EX2] ![](text:image:7382) Select your answer for **Exercise 2**:
if exist("Prob2", "var") && isfield(Prob2, "ID") && Prob2.ID == "Ex2"
    CheckExercise2(Prob2,"Select")  %[control:dropdown:6b625a4]{"position":[26,34]}
else
    warning("Click Generate Problem first.")
end

function CheckExercise2(Prob, Answer)
switch Answer
    case "Select"
        warning("Please select a valid answer.");
    case Prob.CorrectAnswer
        displayFormula(sprintf('"Correct! The torque is %.4f N·m."', Prob.CorrectValue));
        displayFormula('"The vector torque is \\vec{τ} = \\vec{m} \\times \\vec{B}; its magnitude is τ = mB sin(θ)."');
    otherwise
        warning("Incorrect. Remember: τ = mB sin(θ), where m = IA is the magnetic dipole moment.");
        displayFormula('"Hint: First calculate the area, then the dipole moment m = IA, then use τ = mB sin(θ)."');
end
end
%%
%[text] #### Energy Difference
%[text] ![](text:image:149f)![Exercise icon](text:image:0b19) **Exercise 3.** A compass needle with magnetic moment $m = 0.050 \\text{ A\\cdot m}^2$ is in Earth's magnetic field of $B = 5.0 \\times 10^{-5} \\text{ T}$. Calculate the magnitude of the energy difference between the aligned and anti-aligned orientations.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:02f8) **Aligned vs. anti-aligned** ![](text:image:1ec9)
CheckExercise3(0)  %[control:editfield:9d5b71c]{"position":[16,17]} %[control:button:ex3chk]{"position":[18,19]}

function CheckExercise3(Answer)
CorrectAnswer = 2 * 0.050 * 5.0e-5; % ΔU = 2mB
Tolerance = CorrectAnswer * 0.05; % 5% tolerance

if Answer == 0
    warning("Please enter your answer in joules (J).");
elseif abs(Answer - CorrectAnswer) < Tolerance
    displayFormula(sprintf('"Correct! The energy difference is %.2e J."', CorrectAnswer));
    displayFormula('"The energy difference between aligned (U = -mB) and anti-aligned (U = +mB) is ΔU = 2mB."');
else
    warning("Incorrect. Remember: U = -mB cos(θ). What are the energies at θ = 0° and θ = 180°?");
    displayFormula('"Hint: Calculate U at both orientations and find the difference: ΔU = U(180°) - U(0°) = 2mB."');
end
end
%%
%[text] ## Optional Exploration: The Magnetic Field of a Dipole
%[text] The field equation below is a compact vector form for the far-field of a magnetic dipole. The main introductory takeaway is simpler: a dipole field falls off as $1/r^3$, and the field on the dipole axis is twice as large as the field in the equatorial plane at the same distance.
%[text] At points far from the dipole compared to its size, the field can be written as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\vec{B}(\\vec{r}) = \\frac{\\mu\_0}{4\\pi r^3} \\left( 3(\\vec{m} \\cdot \\hat{r})\\hat{r} - \\vec{m} \\right) \\qquad (5)$
%[text] Where:
%[text] - $\\vec{B}(\\vec{r})$ is the magnetic field at position $\\vec{r}$ (in T),
%[text] - $\\mu\_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\text{ T\\cdot m/A}$ is the permeability of free space,
%[text] - $r = |\\vec{r}|$ is the distance from the dipole (in m),
%[text] - $\\hat{r} = \\vec{r}/r$ is the unit vector pointing from the dipole to the field point,
%[text] - $\\vec{m}$ is the magnetic dipole moment (in A·m²). \
%[text] The following equations give the magnitude $B = |\\vec{B}|$ of the field in two important special cases. Along the axis of the dipole ($\\theta = 0\\degree $ or $180\\degree $), Equation (5) simplifies to:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ B = \\frac{\\mu\_0}{4\\pi} \\frac{2m}{r^3} \\qquad (6)$
%[text] In the equatorial plane ($\\theta = 90\\degree $), it becomes:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ B = \\frac{\\mu\_0}{4\\pi} \\frac{m}{r^3} \\qquad (7)$
%[text] Notice that the dipole field falls off as $1/r^3$, which is faster than the $1/r^2$ falloff of a point charge's electric field.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:57c1) **Try.** Compare how quickly $1/r^2$ and $1/r^3$ fields weaken with distance.
  %[control:button:323e]{"position":[1,2]}

PlotInverseLaws();
%[text] 
%[text] ![Reflect icon](text:image:0031) **Reflect.** The steeper $1/r^3$ falloff is why dipole fields become weak quickly far from the source.

% Compare 1/r^2 and 1/r^3 falloff with percentage decreases at sample distances
function PlotInverseLaws()
    r0 = 1; % reference distance (arbitrary units)
    r = linspace(0.5, 6, 500); % radial range (avoid zero)

    f2 = (r0 ./ r).^2; % 1/r^2
    f3 = (r0 ./ r).^3; % 1/r^3

    % Create modern figure
    fig = figure(Name="Inverse Square vs Inverse Cube Law", Color="w", Position=[100 100 900 600]);
    ax = axes(Parent=fig);
    hold(ax, "on");
    grid(ax, "on");
    box(ax, "on");
    ax.YScale = "log";

    % Plot curves with modern styling
    p2 = semilogy(ax, r, f2, LineWidth=2.5, Color=[0 0.4470 0.7410], DisplayName="1/r²");
    p3 = semilogy(ax, r, f3, LineWidth=2.5, Color=[0.8500 0.3250 0.0980], ...
                  LineStyle="--", DisplayName="1/r³");

    % Labels and title
    xlabel(ax, "Distance (r/r₀)", FontSize=13, FontWeight="bold");
    ylabel(ax, "Relative Field Strength (log scale)", FontSize=13, FontWeight="bold");
    title(ax, "Inverse Square Law vs Inverse Cube Law", FontSize=15, FontWeight="bold");

    % Modern legend
    legend(ax, Location="northeast", FontSize=12, Box="off");

    % Set axis limits
    xlim(ax, [min(r) max(r)]);
    ylim(ax, [min([f3,f2]) max([f2(1),f3(1)])]);

    % Annotate percentage decreases at specified multiples of r0
    multiples = [2, 5];
    markers = {'o', 'square'};
    markerSizes = [10, 9];

    for k = 1:numel(multiples)
        rr = multiples(k);
        v2 = (r0/rr)^2;
        v3 = (r0/rr)^3;

        pct2 = 100*(1 - v2);
        pct3 = 100*(1 - v3);

        % Plot markers
        color2 = p2.Color;
        color3 = p3.Color;

        semilogy(ax, rr, v2, markers{k}, MarkerSize=markerSizes(k), ...
                 Color=color2, MarkerFaceColor=color2, ...
                 HandleVisibility="off");
        semilogy(ax, rr, v3, markers{k}, MarkerSize=markerSizes(k), ...
                 Color=color3, MarkerFaceColor=color3, ...
                 HandleVisibility="off");

        % Add text annotations
        yPos2 = v2 * 1.4;
        yPos3 = v3 * 0.6;

        txt2 = sprintf("%.0f%% drop\nat %d×r₀", pct2, rr);
        txt3 = sprintf("%.1f%% drop\nat %d×r₀", pct3, rr);

        text(ax, rr, yPos2, txt2, Color=color2, FontSize=10, ...
             FontWeight="bold", HorizontalAlignment="center", ...
             BackgroundColor=[1 1 1 0.85], EdgeColor=color2);
        text(ax, rr, yPos3, txt3, Color=color3, FontSize=10, ...
             FontWeight="bold", HorizontalAlignment="center", ...
             BackgroundColor=[1 1 1 0.85], EdgeColor=color3);
    end

    % Modern axis styling
    ax.FontSize = 11;
    ax.LineWidth = 1.2;
    ax.GridAlpha = 0.15;
    ax.MinorGridAlpha = 0.05;
end


%%
%[text] ### Further Exploration: Earth as a Magnetic Dipole
%[text] Earth itself acts approximately like a giant magnetic dipole. Earth's magnetic field can be modeled as arising from a magnetic dipole at the center with moment:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ m\_{\\text{Earth}} \\approx 8.0 \\times 10^{22} \\text{ A\\cdot m}^2$
%[text] This is what makes compasses work, the compass needle (a small magnetic dipole) experiences a torque that aligns it with Earth's magnetic field. Interestingly, the magnetic south pole is near the geographic north pole, which is why the "north-seeking" end of a compass points north!
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:9e5d)
%[text] ![](text:image:35f7)
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:474a)![Reflect icon](text:image:239a) **Reflect.** Earth's magnetic field helps navigation and also shapes auroras. Charged particles from the Sun can follow magnetic field lines toward high latitudes, where collisions with atoms in the upper atmosphere emit visible light.
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:2014)![Reflect icon](text:image:7c9b) **Reflect.** Notice how the field strength and inclination change with latitude. At the equator, the field is mostly horizontal (tangential), while at the poles it is mostly vertical (radial). This is why compass needles need to be balanced differently depending on where you are on Earth.
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] In this script, you've explored the fundamental properties and behavior of magnetic dipoles:
%[text] - **Magnetic dipole moment** $\\vec{m} = IA\\hat{n}$ characterizes the strength and orientation of a magnetic dipole
%[text] - **Torque** $\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{m} \\times \\vec{B}$ causes dipoles to align with external fields
%[text] - **Potential energy** $U = -\\vec{m} \\cdot \\vec{B}$ is minimized when aligned, explaining why alignment occurs
%[text] - **Magnetic field of a dipole** falls off as $1/r^3$, faster than point charge fields
%[text] - Real applications include compasses, MRI, data storage, and Earth's magnetism \
%[text] These concepts are the bridge to [DC motor behavior](file:.\\DCmotor.m), magnetic data storage, medical imaging, and the atomic-scale magnetic dipoles that appear in quantum mechanics.
%[text] 
%[text] 
